  m_Name: SparkAIKnowledge
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ProjectSpark.AI.SparkAIKnowledge
  databaseId: project_spark_knowledge
  entries:
  - id: circuit
    title: Circuit
    category: Circuit
    measurement: A circuit can be examined by measuring voltage between two points,
      current through a branch, and resistance when the circuit is de-energized and
      isolated as required. Use a voltmeter or multimeter in voltage mode in parallel
      with the points being tested. Measure current with an ammeter in series with
      the branch. Before measuring resistance or continuity, disconnect the power
      and ensure stored energy is safely discharged. Compare measurements with the
      expected circuit behavior to identify open paths, incorrect connections, or
      unexpected voltage drops.
    aliases:
    - electrical circuit
    - basic circuit
    - circuit basics
    definition: An electrical circuit is a connected path containing a source and
      electrical components that allows electrical energy to be transferred through
      the circuit.
    explanation: A basic circuit needs a source and a continuous electrical path
      through the connected components and back to the other terminal of the source.
    example: A battery connected to an LED through suitable wiring and a current-limiting
      resistor forms a basic electrical circuit when the path returns to the battery.
    importantFacts:
    - A circuit needs an electrical source.
    - A circuit needs connected electrical paths.
    - A complete circuit provides a path between the source terminals.
    - Components must be electrically connected, not merely physically close.
    - An open path prevents normal current flow.
    misconception: A group of components is not automatically a working circuit.
      The components must form a usable electrical path.
    safety: Use the low-voltage Project Spark environment while learning circuit
      fundamentals. Never experiment with mains electricity.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - electrical_source
    - circuit_path
    - wire
    - open_circuit
    - closed_circuit
    - short_circuit
  - id: electrical_source
    title: Electrical Source
    category: Source
    measurement: Measure a source's output voltage by setting a multimeter to the
      appropriate voltage mode and connecting the probes across its positive and
      negative output terminals. For a DC source, use DC voltage mode and observe
      the polarity of the reading. Measure source current only with an appropriate
      current-measurement setup in series with a load, following the meter and source
      instructions. Never connect a current-mode meter directly across a voltage
      source. Check the source's rated voltage and current limits before testing.
    aliases:
    - source
    - power source
    - voltage source
    - battery
    - power supply
    definition: An electrical source provides a potential difference that can drive
      current through a suitable electrical circuit.
    explanation: A source establishes an electrical potential difference between
      its terminals. When a suitable closed path is connected, this potential difference
      can drive current through the circuit.
    example: A battery or laboratory DC power supply can provide voltage to a circuit.
    importantFacts:
    - A source provides electrical potential difference.
    - A source has electrical terminals.
    - Voltage can exist without current flowing.
    - Current requires a suitable electrical path.
    - A source does not automatically cause current when its circuit is open.
    misconception: Having voltage at a source does not mean that current is automatically
      flowing. Current depends on the connected circuit.
    safety: Use low-voltage training sources in Project Spark. Never connect Project
      Spark circuits to mains electricity.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - circuit_path
    - polarity
    - short_circuit
  - id: circuit_path
    title: Circuit Path
    category: Circuit
    measurement: Use a multimeter in voltage mode to measure voltage across individual
      components and connection points. To check continuity of a path, disconnect
      power first, isolate the path where necessary, and use continuity mode according
      to the meter instructions. A continuity beep indicates a sufficiently low-resistance
      path through the tested section, not necessarily that the entire circuit is
      correctly connected. To measure branch current, use a suitable ammeter connected
      in series. Compare readings at different points to help locate breaks, unexpected
      voltage drops, or incorrect connections.
    aliases:
    - path
    - electrical path
    - current path
    - circuit route
    definition: A circuit path is the connected electrical route between the terminals
      of a source through the circuit components.
    explanation: For current to flow in a basic circuit, there must be a continuous
      electrical path from one source terminal through the connected components and
      back to the other source terminal.
    example: A wire from a battery positive terminal to an LED, followed by a return
      connection from the LED to the battery negative terminal, creates a complete
      path.
    importantFacts:
    - Current requires a suitable electrical path.
    - A path can contain multiple components.
    - A broken connection creates an open circuit.
    - The complete path normally connects both source terminals through the circuit.
    misconception: Connecting only one side of a component to a source does not create
      a complete circuit.
    safety: Build and test circuit paths using Project Spark's low-voltage training
      environment.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - electrical_source
    - wire
    - open_circuit
    - closed_circuit
    - short_circuit
  - id: polarity
    title: Polarity
    category: Fundamentals
    measurement: Measure DC polarity by placing the red probe on the point being
      tested as positive and the black probe on the reference point. A positive voltage
      reading means the red probe is at a higher potential than the black probe;
      a negative reading means it is at a lower potential. For a polarized component
      such as an LED, identify its anode and cathode from its markings or documentation
      before connecting it. Do not rely on a voltage reading alone to prove that
      a component is correctly connected or operating.
    aliases:
    - positive
    - negative
    - positive terminal
    - negative terminal
    - anode
    - cathode
    definition: Polarity identifies the positive and negative sides of an electrical
      source or polarized component.
    explanation: Polarity tells us which terminals have positive and negative electrical
      relationships. Polarized components such as LEDs and diodes can require their
      terminals to be connected with the correct polarity.
    example: An LED has an anode and cathode. Connecting the source with the appropriate
      polarity allows the LED to operate when the rest of the circuit is complete.
    importantFacts:
    - Positive and negative terminals have different polarity.
    - LEDs are polarized components.
    - An LED has an anode and a cathode.
    - Polarity can determine whether a polarized component operates.
    - Polarity does not by itself guarantee current flow.
    misconception: Positive and negative terminals are not interchangeable on polarized
      components.
    safety: Use low-voltage Project Spark circuits when learning polarity.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - electrical_source
    - circuit_path
    - led
  - id: open_circuit
    title: Open Circuit
    category: Circuit
    measurement: To investigate an open circuit, use voltage mode to measure across
      the suspected break while the circuit is energized only if it is safe and within
      the meter's ratings. An open switch or broken path may have a voltage across
      it, but the reading depends on the surrounding circuit. To check continuity,
      turn off and disconnect the power, safely discharge stored energy, and isolate
      the tested section as needed. Use continuity or resistance mode only on a de-energized
      circuit. A continuity test can help identify a broken wire, open switch, or
      disconnected terminal.
    aliases:
    - broken circuit
    - disconnected circuit
    - broken path
    - interrupted circuit
    definition: An open circuit is a circuit with a broken or interrupted electrical
      path that prevents normal current flow through that path.
    explanation: An open switch, disconnected wire, or missing connection can interrupt
      the circuit. Voltage may still exist across the open portion even though current
      through that path is effectively zero.
    example: Opening a switch in a simple battery and lamp circuit interrupts the
      path and stops normal current flow.
    importantFacts:
    - An open circuit interrupts the electrical path.
    - An ideal open circuit has zero current.
    - Voltage can exist across an open circuit.
    - An open switch can create an open circuit.
    - A disconnected wire can create an open circuit.
    misconception: An open circuit does not necessarily mean that there is no voltage
      anywhere in the circuit.
    safety: Use Project Spark's low-voltage environment when experimenting with open
      and closed circuits.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - circuit_path
    - open_circuit
    - electrical_source
  - id: closed_circuit
    title: Closed Circuit
    category: Circuit
    measurement: To check a closed circuit, measure the source voltage across its
      output terminals and measure voltage across the intended load. A working, energized
      circuit may show a voltage across the load, depending on the circuit design.
      Measure current by inserting a suitable ammeter in series with the load or
      branch. With power disconnected, continuity mode can help check whether a switch
      or wire provides a conductive path. A closed path alone does not guarantee
      current flow; the source, load, and all connections must also be suitable.
    aliases:
    - complete circuit
    - completed circuit
    - closed path
    - complete path
    definition: A closed circuit has a continuous electrical path that allows current
      to flow when a source and suitable components are present.
    explanation: A closed circuit provides a complete path between the source terminals
      through the connected components. Whether significant current flows depends
      on the source and the electrical properties of the circuit.
    example: Closing a switch in a battery and lamp circuit completes the path and
      allows current to flow through the lamp.
    importantFacts:
    - A closed path can allow current flow.
    - A closed switch normally provides a conducting path.
    - A closed circuit still needs a source to produce current.
    - Circuit resistance affects current.
    - A closed circuit does not mean unlimited current.
    misconception: A closed circuit does not automatically mean that a large current
      is flowing. The source and circuit resistance determine the current.
    safety: Use low-voltage Project Spark circuits when learning about closed circuits.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - circuit_path
    - electrical_source
    - open_circuit
  - id: short_circuit
    title: Short Circuit
    category: Safety
    measurement: A suspected short circuit must be investigated cautiously. Turn
      off the source and disconnect power before using resistance or continuity mode.
      Safely discharge capacitors and isolate the relevant section where necessary.
      A very low resistance between two points that should not be directly connected
      may indicate a short, but other parallel paths can also produce low readings.
      Do not deliberately create a short to test it. If the source reports overcurrent
      or activates current limiting, disconnect power and inspect the circuit before
      further testing.
    aliases:
    - short
    - electrical short
    - shorted circuit
    definition: A short circuit is an unintended low-resistance path that can allow
      excessive current to flow.
    explanation: A short circuit can bypass a component or load with a very low-resistance
      path. The resulting current can become much larger than intended and may damage
      components or the power source.
    example: Connecting the positive and negative terminals of a source through a
      very low-resistance path can create a short circuit.
    importantFacts:
    - A short circuit has very low resistance.
    - A short circuit can produce excessive current.
    - A short can bypass intended components.
    - Short circuits can damage electrical components.
    - Current depends on voltage and circuit resistance.
    misconception: A short circuit is not simply a circuit that works very well.
      It is an unintended low-resistance path that can cause excessive current.
    safety: Never intentionally create short circuits with real batteries, power
      supplies, or mains electricity. Use Project Spark's simulated environment.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - circuit_path
    - electrical_source
    - wire
  - id: wire
    title: Wire
    category: Component
    measurement: To check a wire, disconnect power and isolate at least one end when
      necessary to avoid parallel paths affecting the reading. Use continuity mode
      to check whether the wire conducts between its two ends, or resistance mode
      to measure its resistance. A good short wire usually has low resistance, but
      the reading depends on wire length, thickness, contact quality, and meter-lead
      resistance. To check its role in an energized circuit, use voltage mode across
      appropriate points. Never use continuity or resistance mode on an energized
      circuit.
    aliases:
    - cable
    - conductor
    - connection wire
    - electrical wire
    definition: A wire is a conductive connection used to electrically connect terminals
      and components in a circuit.
    explanation: A wire provides a relatively low-resistance path between connected
      electrical terminals. Wires allow components to be connected together to form
      circuit paths.
    example: A wire can connect a power supply terminal to a component terminal and
      provide the electrical path needed by the circuit.
    importantFacts:
    - A wire provides an electrical connection.
    - An ideal wire has approximately zero resistance.
    - Real wires have some resistance.
    - Wires can connect components in series or parallel arrangements.
    - A disconnected wire can break a circuit.
    misconception: A wire only creates an electrical connection when its conductive
      ends are actually connected to the intended terminals.
    safety: Use the simulated wiring system in Project Spark while learning electrical
      connections.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - circuit_path
    - open_circuit
    - short_circuit
  - id: led
    title: LED
    category: Component
    measurement: Measure the voltage across an LED using DC voltage mode, with the
      probes placed across its two terminals while it is operating in a suitable
      current-limited circuit. Measure circuit current by placing an appropriate
      ammeter in series with the LED branch, or use a suitable current-measurement
      feature designed for the circuit. A typical LED has a forward voltage that
      depends on its type and operating current. Do not connect an LED directly to
      a voltage source without suitable current limiting. For an isolated LED, use
      the multimeter's diode-test mode only according to the meter instructions.
    aliases:
    - light emitting diode
    - light-emitting diode
    - diode
    - indicator LED
    - light
    definition: An LED, or light-emitting diode, is a semiconductor diode that emits
      light when current flows through it in the appropriate direction.
    explanation: An LED is a polarized semiconductor device. Its anode and cathode
      must be connected with appropriate polarity, and the circuit must provide suitable
      current for normal operation.
    example: A low-voltage source can drive an LED through a suitable current-limiting
      resistor when the LED polarity and circuit connections are correct.
    importantFacts:
    - An LED is a diode.
    - An LED has polarity.
    - An LED has an anode and cathode.
    - Forward operation requires appropriate polarity.
    - Current must flow through the LED for normal light emission.
    - An LED normally requires current limiting.
    - Excessive current can damage an LED.
    - Voltage alone does not guarantee that an LED will light.
    misconception: An LED does not light simply because voltage is present. It needs
      appropriate polarity, a complete current path, and suitable current.
    safety: Use low-voltage Project Spark circuits when learning about LEDs. Do not
      connect an LED directly to an uncontrolled voltage source without appropriate
      current limiting.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - circuit_path
    - polarity
    - resistor
    - electrical_source
  - id: resistor
    title: Resistor
    category: Component
    measurement: To measure resistance, disconnect power, safely discharge stored
      energy, and isolate at least one resistor lead when other circuit paths could
      affect the reading. Select resistance mode and place one probe at each end
      of the resistor. Compare the measured value with its rated resistance and tolerance.
      To measure voltage across an operating resistor, use voltage mode in parallel
      with it. To measure current through it, use an appropriate ammeter in series.
      Never use resistance mode on an energized circuit.
    aliases:
    - resistance
    - current limiting resistor
    - current limiter
    - ohmic resistor
    definition: A resistor is an electrical component that provides resistance and
      opposes the flow of current.
    explanation: A resistor limits current and produces a voltage drop when current
      flows through it. In a simple circuit, resistance, voltage, and current are
      related by Ohm's law.
    example: 'A resistor can be placed in series with an LED to limit the current
      flowing through the LED.

'
    importantFacts:
    - A resistor has resistance.
    - Resistance is measured in ohms.
    - A resistor can limit current.
    - A resistor can produce a voltage drop.
    - A resistor dissipates electrical power as heat.
    - Ohm's law relates voltage, current, and resistance.
    - For a given voltage, increasing resistance generally reduces current in a simple
      circuit.
    misconception: A resistor does not stop all current. It limits current according
      to its resistance and the voltage applied to the circuit.
    safety: 'Use appropriately rated resistors in real circuits. Excessive current
      or power can cause a resistor to overheat.

'
    relatedEntryIds:
    - circuit
    - circuit_path
    - electrical_source
    - led
    - polarity
  - id: voltage
    title: Voltage
    category: Electricity
    measurement: Voltage is measured in volts (V) using a voltmeter or a multimeter
      set to the correct voltage mode. Connect the probes in parallel across the
      two points being compared. Select DC voltage for a DC circuit or the appropriate
      AC voltage mode for an AC circuit. The reading represents the electrical potential
      difference between the red and black probe points. A negative DC reading means
      the red probe is at a lower potential than the black probe. Verify the expected
      voltage and meter rating before testing.
    aliases:
    - electric potential difference
    - potential difference
    - volts
    - voltage drop
    definition: Voltage is the electrical potential difference between two points.
      It represents the energy transferred per unit of electric charge between those
      points.
    explanation: Voltage describes the difference in electric potential between two
      points in a circuit. A voltage source establishes a potential difference between
      its terminals. When a suitable closed circuit is connected, that potential
      difference can drive current. Voltage can exist even when no current flows.
    example: A 5 V battery has a potential difference of 5 volts between its terminals.
      If a suitable load is connected in a complete circuit, current can flow. The
      actual voltage across each component depends on the circuit.
    importantFacts:
    - The unit of voltage is the volt (V).
    - Voltage is measured between two points.
    - A multimeter measures voltage in parallel with the component.
    - Voltage can exist without current flowing.
    misconception: Voltage and current are not the same. Voltage is a potential difference;
      current is the rate of flow of electric charge.
    safety: Learn voltage measurements using low-voltage training circuits. Never
      measure or experiment with mains electricity without appropriate training and
      equipment.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - electrical_source  An electrical source establishes a potential difference
      between its terminals.
    - current  Voltage can drive current through a suitable conductive path.
    - "ohms_law  Ohm\u2019s law relates voltage, current, and resistance."
    - resistor  Voltage across a resistor relates to its current and resistance.
    - power  Voltage and current determine electrical power.
    - circuit_path  A suitable electrical path allows current to flow when a driving
      potential difference is present.
    - open_circuit  Voltage can exist across an open circuit even when current through
      the open path is zero.
    - closed_circuit  A closed circuit can allow current to flow when the source
      and load conditions permit it.
    - short_circuit  A short circuit can produce excessive current, depending on
      source and circuit resistance.
    - polarity  Polarity identifies positive and negative sides and helps establish
      voltage reference direction.
    - led  The voltage across an LED depends on its operating current and electrical
      characteristics.
    - wire  Wires connect circuit terminals; ideal wires have no voltage drop, while
      real wires can have a small drop.
  - id: current
    title: Electric Current
    category: Electricity
    measurement: Electric current is measured in amperes (A) using an ammeter or
      a multimeter configured for current measurement. In a conventional circuit,
      the meter is connected in series with the branch so the current flows through
      the meter. Select the correct current type, input terminal, and range, and
      follow the meter instructions. Never connect a current-mode meter directly
      across a voltage source. If a circuit has a dedicated current sensor or shunt,
      current may also be calculated from the measured shunt voltage and its known
      resistance using I = V / R.
    aliases:
    - current
    - electric current
    - measuring current
    - current measurement
    - ammeter
    definition: Electric current is the rate at which electric charge passes through
      a point in a circuit. Its unit is the ampere (A).
    explanation: Electric current describes how much electric charge passes a point
      per unit of time. It is measured in amperes (A). To measure current in a circuit,
      an ammeter is connected in series so that the circuit current passes through
      the meter. The meter must use the correct current input and range. Never connect
      a current meter directly across a voltage source.
    example: If 0.5 amperes flows through a resistor, an ammeter connected in series
      with that resistor reads 0.5 A.
    importantFacts:
    - Current is measured in amperes (A).
    - One ampere equals one coulomb of charge per second.
    - An ammeter is connected in series to measure branch current.
    - In a simple resistive circuit, increasing voltage increases current if resistance
      stays constant.
    - Increasing resistance reduces current if voltage stays constant.
    - Conventional current direction in an external circuit is from higher potential
      toward lower potential; electron drift in metal conductors is in the opposite
      direction.
    misconception: Voltage and current are not the same. Voltage is the electrical
      potential difference between two points, while current measures the rate of
      electric charge flow. A circuit can have voltage across an open switch or disconnected
      load while current through that open path is zero.
    safety: Use the correct current input and range. Connect the meter in series,
      not directly across a voltage source while measuring current.
    relatedEntryIds:
    - voltage  Voltage can drive current through a suitable circuit.
    - "ohms_law  Ohm\u2019s law relates current to voltage and resistance."
    - resistor  Resistance affects current in a circuit.
    - electrical_source  A source establishes potential difference that can drive
      current.
    - circuit_path  A suitable conductive path is needed for sustained current.
    - open_circuit  An open path interrupts current through that path.
    - closed_circuit  A closed path can permit current when the circuit conditions
      allow it.
    - short_circuit  A low-resistance path can cause excessive current.
    - power  Current and voltage determine electrical power.
  - id: ohms_law
    title: Ohm's Law
    category: Electricity
    measurement: "Ohm's law relates voltage, current, and resistance.\r\n\r\nMain
      formula:\r\nV = I \xD7 R\r\n\r\nCalculate current:\r\nI = V / R\r\n\r\nCalculate
      resistance:\r\nR = V / I\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nA 12 V source is connected across
      a 6 \u03A9 resistor.\r\n\r\nI = V / R\r\nI = 12 / 6\r\nI = 2 A\r\n\r\nThe current
      is 2 amperes.\r\n\r\nV = voltage in volts (V)\r\nI = current in amperes (A)\r\nR
      = resistance in ohms (\u03A9)\r\n\r\nFor a fixed resistance, increasing voltage
      increases current.\r\nFor a fixed voltage, increasing resistance decreases
      current.\r\n\r\nThis relationship applies to ohmic components when their resistance
      remains approximately constant. Not all electronic components have a linear
      voltage-current relationship."
    aliases:
    - ohm's law
    - ohms law
    - ohm law
    - relationship between voltage current and resistance
    - calculate current
    - calculate voltage
    - calculate resistance
    definition: "Ohm\u2019s law states that the current through an ohmic conductor
      is equal to the voltage across it divided by its resistance, provided its physical
      conditions remain constant."
    explanation: "Ohm\u2019s law relates voltage, current, and resistance in a circuit.
      Its formula is:\n\nV=I\xD7R\n\nRearranging the formula gives:\n\nI=\nR\nV\n\t\u200B\n\nR=\nI\nV\n\t\u200B\n\n\nHere,
      V is voltage in volts, I is current in amperes, and R is resistance in ohms.
      For a fixed resistance, higher voltage produces higher current. For a fixed
      voltage, higher resistance produces lower current. The relationship applies
      to ohmic components when their resistance remains constant; not all electronic
      components follow a linear voltage-current relationship."
    example: "A 10 \u03A9 resistor connected across an ideal 5 V source has a current
      of:\n\nI=\n10\n5\n\t\u200B\n\n=0.5 A\n\nIf the voltage rises to 10 V and resistance
      remains 10 \u03A9, current rises to 1 A."
    importantFacts:
    - Voltage is measured in volts (V).
    - Current is measured in amperes (A).
    - "Resistance is measured in ohms (\u03A9). if resistance stays constant."
    - For fixed resistance, doubling voltage doubles current.nstant.
    - For fixed voltage, doubling resistance halves current. potential toward lower
      potential; electron drift in metal conductors is in the opposite direction.
    - Real components may heat up, changing their resistance.
    misconception: "Ohm\u2019s law does not mean every component has constant resistance.
      Incandescent lamps, diodes, LEDs, and many other components can have nonlinear
      voltage-current behavior. Use the relationship with the appropriate component
      model and operating conditions."
    safety: "Use Ohm\u2019s law to plan low-voltage training circuits and check component
      ratings. Always consider resistor power dissipation using P=VI and never use
      calculations as a reason to experiment with mains electricity."
    relatedEntryIds:
    - voltage  Voltage can drive current through a suitable circuit.
    - "ohms_law  Ohm\u2019s law relates current to voltage and resistance."
    - resistor  Resistance affects current in a circuit.
    - electrical_source  A source establishes potential difference that can drive
      current.
    - circuit_path  A suitable conductive path is needed for sustained current.
    - open_circuit  An open path interrupts current through that path.
    - closed_circuit  A closed path can permit current when the circuit conditions
      allow it.
    - short_circuit  A low-resistance path can cause excessive current.
    - power  Current and voltage determine electrical power.
